the target language is: prolog.

% Unknowns inside a universal ("for all cases in which ...").
%
% A case whose CONDITION holds only by assuming an unknown is still a case: its
% consequent has to hold too, and the assumption has to be reported. Skipping
% such a case claimed the universal held while a case that may well exist went
% unchecked, and returned no unknowns to say so — a confident answer with no
% signal, the same defect that used to hide unknowns inside aggregates.

the templates are:
    *a person* is on the list; unknown.
    *a person* is enrolled.
    *a person* has paid.
    every enrolled person on the list has paid.

the knowledge base forall_unknowns includes:

every enrolled person on the list has paid
    if for all cases in which
        a person is enrolled
        and the person is on the list
        it is the case that
        the person has paid.

% Ann is the only enrolled person and she has paid. Whether she is on the list
% was never stated, so the universal holds only on that assumption — and says so.
scenario open is:
    ann is enrolled.
    ann has paid.
    paid expects answers ["every enrolled person on the list has paid"]
        and unknowns ["ann is on the list"].

% Ben is enrolled and has not paid. His being on the list is exactly as open a
% question as Ann's, so his case must be checked, not skipped: the universal
% must not be claimed.
scenario counterexample is:
    ann is enrolled.
    ann has paid.
    ben is enrolled.
    paid expects answers [] and unknowns [].

% List membership stated outright for the one enrolled person: nothing is left
% to assume.
scenario settled is:
    ann is enrolled.
    ann has paid.
    ann is on the list.
    paid expects answers ["every enrolled person on the list has paid"] and unknowns [].

% Nobody is enrolled: the universal is vacuously true, with nothing assumed.
scenario vacuous is:
    paid expects answers ["every enrolled person on the list has paid"] and unknowns [].

query paid is:
    every enrolled person on the list has paid.
